      "✅☑Beats 100% || [C++/Java/Python/JavaScript] || 2 Approaches || EXPLAINED🔥 MarkSPhilip31 8481 6738 Dec 20, 2023 C++ Java Python3 JavaScript 3+ PLEASE UPVOTE IF IT HELPED \n \n Approaches \n (Also explained in the code) \n Approach 1(Sorting) \n \n Sorting Points: \n \n \n The function begins by sorting the  points  vector based on the x-coordinate of each point. This is done using  sort(points.begin(), points.end()) . \n \n \n Calculating Maximum Width: \n \n \n It then iterates through the sorted points. \n For each pair of consecutive points, it calculates the difference in x-coordinates ( points[i][0] - points[i-1][0] ). \n It updates the  diff  variable to hold the maximum difference encountered while traversing the sorted points. \n \n \n Returning the Maximum Width: \n \n \n Finally, it returns the maximum  diff , which represents the maximum width between two consecutive x-coordinates among the sorted points. \n \n Complexity \n \n \n Time complexity: \n O(nlogn)O(nlogn) O ( n l o g n ) \n \n \n Space complexity: \n O(logn)O(logn) O ( l o g n ) \n \n \n Code \n C++ Java Python3 JavaScript var   maxWidthOfVerticalArea   =   function ( points )   { \n     points . sort ( ( a ,  b )   =>  a [ 0 ]   -  b [ 0 ] ) ; \n      let  diff  =   0 ; \n      for   ( let  i  =   1 ;  i  <  points . length ;  i ++ )   { \n         diff  =   Math . max ( diff ,  points [ i ] [ 0 ]   -  points [ i  -   1 ] [ 0 ] ) ; \n      } \n      return  diff ; \n } ; \n \n \n \n Approach 2(With Sets) \n \n The function  maxWidthOfVerticalArea  takes a vector of vectors points representing coordinates. \n It utilizes a set  xCoordinates  to store unique x-coordinates from the input points. \n Iterates through each point, extracting its x-coordinate and inserts it into the set. \n Initializes variables  maxDiff  to keep track of the maximum difference between x-coordinates and  prevX  to hold the previous x-coordinate. \n Iterates through the set of x-coordinates:\n \n Updates  maxDiff  by finding the maximum difference between the current x-coordinate and the previous x-coordinate. \n Updates  prevX  with the current x-coordinate for the next iteration. \n \n \n Returns the  maxDiff , which represents the maximum width of the vertical area. \n \n Complexity \n \n \n Time complexity: \n O(nlogn)O(nlogn) O ( n l o g n ) \n \n \n Space complexity: \n O(n)O(n) O ( n ) \n \n \n Code \n C++ Java Python3 JavaScript \n var   maxWidthOfVerticalArea   =   function ( points )   { \n      let  xCoordinates  =   new   Set ( ) ; \n     \n      for   ( let  point  of  points )   { \n         xCoordinates . add ( point [ 0 ] ) ; \n      } \n     \n      let  maxDiff  =   0 ; \n      let  prevX  =   null ; \n     \n      for   ( let  x  of   [ ... xCoordinates ] . sort ( ( a ,  b )   =>  a  -  b ) )   { \n          if   ( prevX  !==   null )   { \n             maxDiff  =   Math . max ( maxDiff ,  x  -  prevX ) ; \n          } \n         prevX  =  x ; \n      } \n     \n      return  maxDiff ; \n } ; \n \n \n PLEASE UPVOTE IF IT HELPED \n \n \n Comments (7) maria_q Dec 21, 2023 There are  several interesting one line solutions  for this task - seems useful. 1 Reply santanu29 Dec 21, 2023 Space complexity for the first(sorting) solution is O(N) as no extra space is used. \nFor the second solution the time complexity of set is O(logN). So the time complexity should be O(logN) 0 Show 1 Replies Reply 666Lucifer666 Dec 21, 2023 how can the space complexity is log n in first case there is no space used 0 Show 2 Replies Reply Neelesh_Aggarwal Dec 21, 2023 The code is still not beating 100% runtime, Is there any other more optimized approach ? 0 Show 1 Replies Reply cs_iitian Dec 20, 2023 Video Solution - 4 Different Approach with Detailed Analysis \n \n Sorting \n Sorting x-axis array \n TreeSet \n PriorityQueue \n \n Read more 0 Reply PratikshaHire Dec 20, 2023 class Solution { \npublic: \nint maxWidthOfVerticalArea(vector<vector>& points) { \nset s; \nfor(int i = 0; i < points.size(); i++) \n{ \ns.insert(points[i][0]); //insert only the x co-ordinates \n} \nint diff = 0; \nfor(auto it = ++s.begin(); it != s.end(); it++) \n{ \nauto prev_it = --it; \ndiff = max(diff, (*it) - (*prev_it)); \n} \nreturn diff; \n} \n}; \nI used iterator for pointing to the previous element. Is it invalid or smth because it gives TLE? Read more 0 Show 1 Replies Reply MrAke Dec 20, 2023 more language solution is  here 0 Show 1 Replies Reply",
      "✅Beats 100% - C++, JAVA, Python, JavaScript || Easiest Solutions with explanation || 2 Approaches Vivekkrsk 530 2088 Dec 20, 2023 C++ Java Python JavaScript 4+ \n Please Upvote If It Is Helpfull \n \n Approach \n\n \n \n Sort Points by X-Coordinates:  The code begins by sorting the  points  vector based on their x-coordinates. This ensures that points are arranged from left to right, which is crucial for calculating widths accurately. \n \n \n Initialize Maximum Width:  It initializes  ans  to 0, which will store the maximum width found so far. \n \n \n Iterate Through Sorted Points:  A loop iterates through the sorted points, starting from the second point. This is because the width is calculated between pairs of points. \n \n \n Calculate Width and Update Maximum:  For each pair of consecutive points (current and previous), it calculates the width by subtracting the x-coordinate of the previous point from the x-coordinate of the current point. This difference represents the horizontal distance between them. The  ans  variable is updated if this width is greater than the current maximum width. \n \n \n Return Maximum Width:  After iterating through all pairs, the code returns the  ans  variable, which holds the largest width found among all pairs of points in the vertical area. \n \n \n Complexity \n \n Time complexity:O(nlogn) \n \n\n \n Space complexity:O(1) \n \n\n Code \n C++ Java Python JavaScript Ruby go  \n var   maxWidthOfVerticalArea   =   function ( points )   { \n     points . sort ( ( a ,  b )   =>  a [ 0 ]   -  b [ 0 ] ) ;   // Sort by x-coordinates \n \n      let  maxWidth  =   0 ; \n      for   ( let  i  =   1 ;  i  <  points . length ;  i ++ )   { \n         maxWidth  =   Math . max ( maxWidth ,  points [ i ] [ 0 ]   -  points [ i  -   1 ] [ 0 ] ) ; \n      } \n \n      return  maxWidth ; \n } ; \n Approach \n\n Here's the approach in 5 points: \n \n \n Collect Unique X-Coordinates: \n \n Creates a  set  named  uniqueX  to store unique x-coordinates from the input  points . \n Iterates through  points  and inserts each point's x-coordinate into  uniqueX . \n \n \n \n Initialize Variables: \n \n Sets  maxDifference  to 0 to track the largest width found so far. \n Sets  previousX  to  INT_MIN  to represent the absence of a previous x-coordinate. \n \n \n \n Iterate Through Unique X-Coordinates: \n \n Iterates through each distinct x-coordinate  x  in the  uniqueX  set. \n \n \n \n Calculate Width and Update Maximum: \n \n If  previousX  is not  INT_MIN  (meaning a previous x-coordinate exists), it calculates the width between the current and previous x-coordinates ( x - previousX ). \n Updates  maxDifference  if the calculated width is larger than the current maximum. \n \n \n \n Return Maximum Width: \n \n After processing all unique x-coordinates, the code returns  maxDifference , representing the largest width found in the vertical area. \n \n \n \n Complexity \n \n Time complexity:O(nlogn) \n \n\n \n Space complexity:O(n) \n \n\n Code \n C++ Java Python JavaScript Ruby go  \n function   maxWidthOfVerticalArea ( points )   { \n    const  uniqueX  =   new   Set ( ) ; \n \n    for   ( const  point  of  points )   { \n     uniqueX . add ( point [ 0 ] ) ; \n    } \n \n    let  maxDifference  =   0 ; \n    let  previousX  =   Number . MIN_SAFE_INTEGER ; \n \n    for   ( const  x  of  uniqueX )   { \n      if   ( previousX  !==   Number . MIN_SAFE_INTEGER )   { \n       maxDifference  =   Math . max ( maxDifference ,  x  -  previousX ) ; \n      } \n     previousX  =  x ; \n    } \n \n    return  maxDifference ; \n } \n \n \n <img src=\"https://assets.leetcode.com/users/images/4f5dfcd0-7ad7-4ca4-a526-407441d3e7f8_1703122317.40577.png\" alt=\"imgleetcode.png\"> Comments (5) niits Dec 20, 2023 Hey please stop downvoting my post. It's obvious. 11 Reply maria_q Dec 21, 2023 There are  several interesting one line solutions  for this task - seems useful. 1 Reply MarkSPhilip31 Dec 20, 2023 Congratulations mate u copied my solution well. \nGuys give it a check. Beats 100%  https://leetcode.com/problems/widest-vertical-area-between-two-points-containing-no-points/solutions/4432795/beats-100-c-java-python-javascript-2-approaches-explained/?envType=daily-question&envId=2023-12-21 1 Reply alizhadigerov Dec 21, 2023 why people are fighting each other for upvotes? :D 0 Show 1 Replies Reply cs_iitian Dec 20, 2023 Video Solution - 4 Different Approach with Detailed Analysis \n \n Sorting \n Sorting x-axis array \n TreeSet \n PriorityQueue \n \n Read more 0 Reply",
      "🚀 Simplest Solution on Leetcode | 5 Lines of Code | Just Visit Once 🔥🔥 Sanket_Deshmukh_24 886 1099 Dec 20, 2023 C++ Java Python C 4+ Intuition 🤔 \n The problem asks us to find the widest vertical area between two points on a 2D plane, such that no other points lie inside this area. The width of the area is determined by the difference in x-coordinates of two consecutive points when the points are sorted. We want to maximize this width. \n <img src=\"https://assets.leetcode.com/users/images/6526ba4b-f963-4a27-869a-cedf79c47f12_1703127622.6237767.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2023-12-21 082420.png\"> \n\n Approach 🚀 \n \n Sort the points : To find the width, we first need to sort the points based on their x-coordinates. \n Calculate Widths : Iterate through the sorted points and calculate the width between each pair of consecutive points. Keep track of the maximum width encountered so far. \n Maximize Width : Update the maximum width whenever a wider vertical area is found. \n \n\n Complexity \n \n \n Time Complexity: ⏰ \nThe dominant operation is the sorting of the points, which takes O(n log n) time, where n is the number of points. The subsequent iteration takes O(n) time. Therefore, the overall time complexity is O(n log n). \n \n \n Space Complexity: 🧠 \nThe space complexity is O(1) since we are using a constant amount of extra space (no additional data structures) regardless of the input size. \n \n \n Code \n \n Sorting:   sort(points.begin(), points.end())  sorts the points based on their x-coordinates. \n Width Calculation:  Iterate through the sorted points, calculating the width between consecutive points using  currX - prevX . \n Maximum Width Update:   ans = max(ans, currX - prevX)  updates the maximum width whenever a wider vertical area is found. \n Input/Output Optimization:   ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false)  and related lines optimize input/output for faster execution. \n \n class   Solution   { \n public : \n      int   maxWidthOfVerticalArea ( vector < vector < int >> &  points )   { \n         ios_base :: sync_with_stdio ( false ) ; \n         cin . tie ( 0 ) ; \n         cout . tie ( 0 ) ; \n         \n          sort ( points . begin ( ) , points . end ( ) ) ; \n          int  ans  =   0 ; \n          for ( int  i  =   1 ;  i  <  points . size ( ) ;  i ++ ) \n          { \n              int  prevX  =  points [ i  -   1 ] [ 0 ] ; \n              int  currX  =  points [ i ] [ 0 ] ; \n             ans  =   max ( ans ,  currX  -  prevX ) ; \n          } \n          return  ans ; \n      } \n } ; \n <img src=\"https://assets.leetcode.com/users/images/e040b6a5-0055-4063-9fd9-c10e42f1ae96_1703127648.940425.png\" alt=\"lc1.png\"> Comments (2) 9582anupam Dec 21, 2023 Sorting will take N LogN time. So overall time complexity should be O(N LogN) 0 Reply maria_q Dec 21, 2023 There are  several interesting one line solutions  for this task - seems useful. 0 Reply"
