      "Three lines of code for javascript solution Azizjon003 118 3830 Apr 14, 2023 JavaScript Complexity \n \n Time complexity:99% \n \n\n \n Space complexity:88% \n \n\n Code \n  \n const   runningSum   =   nums   =>   { \n    let  sum  =   0 ; \n    return  nums . map ( num   =>  sum  +=  num ) ; \n } ; Comments (4) brycancode Aug 30, 2023 can you explain how you did this? 3 Show 1 Replies Reply brycancode Aug 30, 2023 impressive btw 2 Reply hdoubleu Nov 02, 2023 Why does the value of sum increase? 0 Show 3 Replies Reply mijac0624 Sep 24, 2023 impressive 0 Reply",
      "Simple and nice approach, 0 ms runtime, beats 100% techyguyaditya 45 18217 Feb 23, 2023 C++ Java Python3 JavaScript 1+ Intuition \n\n I thought of just keep on adding sum and return. That gave me error, because I didn't read the question properly xD. I just added 2 extra lines of code to it. \n Approach \n\n \n First initialize total sum and return vector. \n Then, iterate each element in nums vector. \n Keep on adding sum to the iterative value and keep on pushing new sum value to return array \n Return the array. WOW!!! Profit!!! Nice :) \n \n Pls upvote this multilingual solution \n Code \n C++ \n class   Solution   { \n public : \n     vector < int >   runningSum ( vector < int > &  nums )   { \n          int  sums  =   0 ; \n         vector < int >  retSums ; \n          for ( auto  i  :  nums )   { \n             sums  +=  i ; \n             retSums . push_back ( sums ) ; \n          } \n          return  retSums ; \n      } \n } ; \n Python \n class   Solution : \n      def   runningSum ( self ,  nums :  List [ int ] )   - >  List [ int ] : \n         sums = 0 \n         retSum = [ ] \n          for  i  in  nums : \n             sums += i\n             retSum . append ( sums ) \n          return  retSum \n Rust \n impl   Solution   { \n      pub   fn   running_sum ( nums :   Vec < i32 > )   ->   Vec < i32 >   { \n          let   mut  sum  =   0 ; \n          let   mut  retValue : Vec < i32 >   =   Vec :: new ( ) ; \n          for  num  in  nums  { \n             sum  +=  num ; \n             retValue . push ( sum ) ; \n          } \n         retValue\n      } \n } \n Javascript \n var   runningSum   =   function ( nums )   { \n      let  sums  =   0 ; \n      let  retSums  =   [ ] ; \n      for ( let  i  of  nums )   { \n         sums  +=  i ; \n         retSums . push ( sums ) ; \n      } \n      return  retSums ; \n } \n Java \n class   Solution   { \n      public   int [ ]   runningSum ( int [ ]  nums )   { \n          int  sums  =   0 ; \n          int [ ]  retSums  =   new   int [ nums . length ] ; \n          for ( int  i  =   0 ;  i  <  nums . length ;  i ++ )   { \n             sums  +=  nums [ i ] ; \n             retSums [ i ]   =  sums ; \n          } \n          return  retSums ; \n      } \n } \n <img src=\"https://assets.leetcode.com/users/images/2d8c6ac2-0d70-4e56-9c24-5d4aee04e91e_1680246415.857811.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2023-03-31 123451.png\"> Comments (7) prathmesh_patil Mar 13, 2023 0ms runtime?? 9 Show 1 Replies Reply piyushcodes2403 Feb 23, 2023 Nice bro you gave your solution in a lot of different languages... 3 Reply guywithimpostersyndrome Apr 19, 2023 Using  append  in your Python code is not efficient since it expands on demand (as soon as it gets full) which takes time and additional memory. \n Instead, pre-defining the result's array size helps. 1 Show 1 Replies Reply Urvash_Shende Feb 23, 2023 Thanks bro, was stuck in this questions but by this i can now easily understand the concept of this question. Upvote to techyguyaditya 1 Show 1 Replies Reply Pisgah Mar 31, 2024 Just solved my second problem by thine guidance. Thanks bro. 0 Reply ebekuedikegerald Jul 02, 2023 great one! 0 Reply 1_Isha Feb 24, 2023 Effort Appreciated 0 Reply"
