      "Faster than 98.32%, memory usage less than 100.00%. 2 lines of code. user7497Tv 51 7399 Mar 22, 2020 JavaScript  \n var   smallerNumbersThanCurrent   =   function ( nums )   { \n      const  sorted  =   [ ... nums ] . sort ( ( a ,  b )   =>  a  -  b ) ; \n      return  nums . map ( num   =>  sorted . indexOf ( num ) ) ; \n } ; Comments (13) origami1024 May 23, 2020 So sad, too bad that .indexOf inside .map makes it O(N^2) 30 Show 1 Replies Reply return-zero May 20, 2021 Surprised one can get away with sorting to achieve 98.32% percent speed up. The cases are not large enough for it to have a measurable impact. Sorting solution is much more expensive than the linear time solution. 7 Reply anuragint Aug 15, 2020 It will work for all the cases other than duplicates! You will need to use lastIndexOf rather than indexOf! 4 Show 3 Replies Reply lunevdev Feb 22, 2022 It doesn't work with this test case: \nInput [5,0,10,0,10,6] \nOutput [4,0,2,0,2,5] \nExpected [2,0,4,0,4,3] 2 Show 2 Replies Reply amandev1 Nov 20, 2022 interviewer will not get satisfied by telling it was 98% faster 0 Reply vishal238128 Jun 25, 2022 @user7497Tv  Could you explain me your code 0 Reply KarlosYe May 05, 2022 I dont understand why you would use [...nums].sort instead of just nums.sort? 0 Show 2 Replies Reply mayliang Mar 01, 2022 I like the idea 0 Reply denik1981 Jun 03, 2021 Thanks for this. Cleaver but at the moment I'm wrintng this this solution is faster ~ 60% of all javascript submissions. 0 Reply AbdulKhader4123 Mar 23, 2021 nums is already an array,.. then why are you using spread? 0 Show 2 Replies Reply",
      "[JavaScript] Clean solution using array sort yuria-n 52 5464 Feb 29, 2020 JavaScript Code (JavaScript): \n  \n function   smallerNumbersThanCurrent ( nums )   { \n    const  sorted  =   Array . from ( nums ) . sort ( ( n1 ,  n2 )   =>  n2  -  n1 ) ; \n    const  map  =   new   Map ( sorted . map ( ( num ,  index )   =>   [ num ,  nums . length   -  index  -   1 ] ) ) ; \n    return  nums . map ( num   =>  map . get ( num ) ) ; \n } \n Complexity: \n Time  O(NlogN) \nSpace  O(N) \n Example: \nnums  [8,1,2,2,3] \n \n Sort  nums  by descending order\n \n sorted  [8,3,2,2,1] \n \n \n Create  map  with counts\n \n map:  {8:4,3:3,2:1,1:0} \n \n \n Return an array with counts stored in  map \n \n result:  [4,0,1,1,3] \n \n \n Comments (5) stevenkitzes Apr 08, 2020 0 Show 2 Replies Reply MianAhmad Dec 10, 2022 i am a beginner js developer can you explain what is the input to map class and how does this outcome comes anyone is welcome to answer \nThanks in advance 0 Reply enthusiastic_learner Dec 28, 2020 Why you have used Array.from , why it is needed please explain it. 0 Show 1 Replies Reply GabeRivera Apr 26, 2020 Sweet solution! 0 Reply rbrand21 Mar 31, 2020 Would this work for arrays that have non-unique numbers? i.e. [7,7,7,7] 0 Show 2 Replies Reply",
      "Faster than 100%. Simple Javascript hashmap solution. derekdevs4days 19 2235 Nov 21, 2022 JavaScript Sorting Algorithm \n input: [8, 1, 2, 2, 3] \n \n declare a new array with the input array sorted in ascending order \n+++ The elements's index shows the amount of numbers that are smaller.+++ \n \n sorted: [1, 2, 2, 3, 8] \n \n iterate through sorted array and set the number and its index to a Map (skip any that already exist in the Map) \n \n map: { 1 => 0, 2 => 1, 3 => 3, 8 => 4 } \n \n iterate through the original input and create a new array with the current number's value from the Map \n \n output: [4, 0, 1, 1, 3] \n  \n var   smallerNumbersThanCurrent   =   function ( nums )   { \n      let  hash  =   new   Map ( ) ; \n      let  sorted  =  nums . slice ( ) . sort ( ( a , b )   =>  a  -  b ) ; \n     sorted . forEach ( ( number ,  idx )   =>  hash . has ( number ) ?   null :  hash . set ( number ,  idx ) ) ; \n      return  nums . map ( x   =>  hash . get ( x ) ) ; \n } ; \n <img src=\"https://assets.leetcode.com/users/images/a11f5325-2826-4f0b-8a7c-8d3f9931ef4a_1669076360.1411996.png\" alt=\"image\"> Comments (4) nileshbohra Mar 13, 2023 amazing solution 0 Reply sergey-lypko Dec 01, 2022 perfect 0 Reply user6199cJ Dec 01, 2022 wow 0 Reply lolomg Nov 21, 2022 nice 0 Reply",
      "1365 | JavaScript 1-Line Solution Sporkyy 373 3089 Mar 02, 2020 JavaScript I'm 🤜⎯S⎯T⎯R⎯E⎯T⎯C⎯H⎯I⎯N⎯G⎯🤛 the definition a bit here. \n But, then again, aren't I always? 😜 \n \n Runtime:  92 ms , faster than  84.06%  of JavaScript online submissions \nMemory Usage:  41 MB , less than  17.24%  of JavaScript online submissions \n \n  \n const   smallerNumbersThanCurrent   =   nums   => \n    ( map   =>  nums . map ( n   =>  map . get ( n ) ) ) ( \n      new   Map ( \n        [ ... nums ] \n          . sort ( ( a ,  b )   =>  b  -  a ) \n          . map ( ( val ,  idx )   =>   [ val ,  nums . length   -  idx  -   1 ] ) , \n      ) , \n    ) ; \n Here is the boring multiple-line solution. \n \n Runtime:  92 ms , faster than  85.25%  of JavaScript online submissions \nMemory Usage:  40.9 MB , less than  20.23%  of JavaScript online submissions \n \n  \n const   smallerNumbersThanCurrent   =   nums   =>   { \n    const  map  =   new   Map ( \n      [ ... nums ] \n        . sort ( ( a ,  b )   =>  b  -  a ) \n        . map ( ( val ,  idx )   =>   [ val ,  nums . length   -  idx  -   1 ] ) , \n    ) ; \n    return  nums . map ( n   =>  map . get ( n ) ) ; \n } ; \n Here is another 1-line solution where the  Map  object is created in the \nparameters of the original function. Some people seem to prefer this technique. \n \n Runtime:  92 ms , faster than  85.25%  of JavaScript online submissions \nMemory Usage:  41.1 MB , less than  17.95%  of JavaScript online submissions \n \n  \n   const  smallerNumbersThanCurrent  =   ( \n   nums , \n   map  =   new   Map ( \n      [ ... nums ] \n        . sort ( ( a ,  b )   =>  b  -  a ) \n        . map ( ( val ,  idx )   =>   [ val ,  nums . length   -  idx  -   1 ] ) , \n    ) , \n )   =>  nums . map ( n   =>  map . get ( n ) ) ; \n Creating the  Map  object outside of the  map  function is important for \nperformance reasons. Recreating the  Map  object on every iteration of the  map \nfunction slows execution significantly. \n \n Runtime:  1004 ms , faster than  5.58%  of JavaScript online submissions \nMemory Usage:  45.4 MB , less than  5.05%  of JavaScript online submissions \n \n  \n const   smallerNumbersThanCurrent   =   nums   => \n   nums . map ( n   => \n      new   Map ( \n        [ ... nums ] \n          . sort ( ( a ,  b )   =>  b  -  a ) \n          . map ( ( val ,  idx )   =>   [ val ,  nums . length   -  idx  -   1 ] ) , \n      ) . get ( n ) , \n    ) ; \n And here is the original solution with the unnecessary  reverse  function. \n \n Runtime:  100 ms , faster than  49.01%  of JavaScript online submissions \nMemory Usage:  40.3 MB , less than  14.09%  of JavaScript online submissions \n \n  \n const   smallerNumbersThanCurrent   =   nums   => \n    ( map   =>  nums . map ( n   =>  map . get ( n ) ) ) ( \n      new   Map ( \n        [ ... nums ] \n          . sort ( ( a ,  b )   =>  a  -  b ) \n          . map ( ( val ,  idx )   =>   [ val ,  idx ] ) \n          . reverse ( ) , \n      ) , \n    ) ; Comments (8) Veeraj3103 Jan 26, 2021 what does the [...num] do ? can't we use just num ? 1 Show 3 Replies Reply myildizCH Sep 29, 2022 JS Map works very slow. I think no need to use it for the sake of one line solution. 0 Reply return-zero May 20, 2021 Good clean code but a bit of a stretch to call this one a one liner. 0 Show 1 Replies Reply cloudwolker Mar 07, 2021 I notice that the result second bracket is fed into the first bracket. What is this called? Is this a functional thing? 0 Show 3 Replies Reply betsuni Nov 25, 2020 why  reverse ? 0 Show 2 Replies Reply LenWinkler Sep 23, 2020 Nice! 0 Reply RoyEun May 03, 2020 this is awesome! 0 Reply fbecker11 Apr 22, 2020 Great solution! 0 Reply",
      "JavaScript - Elegant O(n) solution guyariely 16 1485 Aug 22, 2021 JavaScript The key thing to understand here is that the numbers are in the  range of 0 to 100 . \nThis allows us to compute the amount of smaller numbers for every possible number in constant time. \n Let's define:  smaller(n) = amount of numbers smaller than n in nums . \n We start off by initializing two arrays: \n const  frequencies  =   new   Array ( 101 ) . fill ( 0 ) ; \n const  smaller  =   new   Array ( 101 ) . fill ( 0 ) ; \n Then we want to compute what is the frequency of any given 0 <= n <= 100 in the array: \n nums . forEach ( num  = >  frequencies [ num ] ++ ) ; \n Now for the important part: \n We will compute  smaller(n)  for every  0 <= n <= 100 . \n The value of smaller(n) is how many numbers are smaller than the number before it plus the frequency of that number in nums. \n (If that sentence didn't make sense to you, read it again or try playing with some examples. Continue reading after you're comfortable with the idea) \n Notice how this is exactly the definition from the beginning. \n\"how many numbers are smaller than the number before it\" is just  smaller(n-1) . \n So in other words:  smaller(n) = smaller(n-1) + frequency(n-1) \n This is great, we can now just compute smaller(n) on the go: \n   for   ( let  i  =   1 ;  i  <   101 ;  i ++ )   { \n    smaller [ i ]   =  smaller [ i  -   1 ]   +  frequencies [ i  -   1 ] ; \n } \n At last, we actually don't want every smaller(n), just the ones from the nums array, and in the same order. So we will map every num in nums to smaller(num): \n return  nums . map ( num  = >  smaller [ num ] ) ; \n And this is the entire code: \n  \n function   smallerNumbersThanCurrent ( nums )   { \n    const  frequencies  =   new   Array ( 101 ) . fill ( 0 ) ; \n    const  smaller  =   new   Array ( 101 ) . fill ( 0 ) ; \n \n   nums . forEach ( num   =>  frequencies [ num ] ++ ) ; \n \n    for   ( let  i  =   1 ;  i  <   101 ;  i ++ )   { \n     smaller [ i ]   =  smaller [ i  -   1 ]   +  frequencies [ i  -   1 ] ; \n    } \n \n    return  nums . map ( num   =>  smaller [ num ] ) ; \n } \n Side note:  this method is often described as \"dynamic programming\". Basically, we broke down the value of smaller(n) to a simpler sub-problem and used it to optimize the computation of smaller(n). Comments (1) yuliatuzov Jan 12, 2022 yo, this is an awesome solution. I wonder if there other ways to achieve O(n) besides DP. 2 Reply"
