      "Clean JavaScript Solution shimphillip 254 872 Oct 28, 2020 JavaScript // time O(n) space O(n) \n var   decompressRLElist   =   function ( nums )   { \n      let  result  =   [ ] \n     \n      for ( let  i = 0 ;  i < nums . length ;  i += 2 )   { \n          const  frequency  =  nums [ i ] \n          const  value  =  nums [ i + 1 ] \n         \n          // result = [...result, ...new Array(frequency).fill(value)] \n         result . push ( ... new   Array ( frequency ) . fill ( value ) ) \n      } \n     \n      return  result\n } ; \n var   decompressRLElist   =   function ( nums )   { \n      const  result  =   [ ] \n     \n      for ( let  i = 0 ;  i < nums . length ;  i += 2 )   { \n          const  frequency  =  nums [ i ] \n          const  value  =  nums [ i + 1 ] \n         \n          for ( let  j = 0 ;  j < frequency ;  j ++ )   { \n             result . push ( value ) \n          }     \n      } \n     \n      return  result\n } ; Comments (2) ChaseTheCoder Mar 22, 2021 This makes so much sense!  Great use of the spread operator, I now know how to use them.  I always look to your javascript solutions as they are easy to read as a newer programmer. 4 Reply dlande000 May 07, 2021 Does  result.push(...new Array(frequency).fill(value))  really have such a negligible runtime such that this solution is  O(n) ? This Stack Overflow answer thinks that the spread operator runs in O(n):  https://stackoverflow.com/questions/57032373/whats-the-time-complexity-of-javascript-spread-syntax-in-arrays \n Which, if true, means that big O for this is O(n ^ m) (where n is the  nums.length  and m is the largest  of the frequencies), and that's before considering the runtimes of creating and filling a new array. 3 Show 1 Replies Reply",
