      "[JavaScript] Easy to understand - 2 solutions poppinlp 1703 1715 Apr 05, 2021 JavaScript SOLUTION 1 \n We could solve it easily via spliting. \n const   truncateSentence   =   ( s ,  k )   =>  s . split ( ' ' ,  k ) . join ( ' ' ) ; \n SOLUTION 2 \n Let's see if we could have a better performance. \nSince there's no leading or trailing space, and words that are separated by only a single space, so if we want only the first  k  words, we could just count  k  spaces. \nAnd, don't forget to return whole  s  if there are not enough words. \n const   truncateSentence   =   ( s ,  k )   =>   { \n    let  spaceCount  =   0 ; \n    for   ( let  i  =   0 ;  i  <  s . length ;   ++ i )   { \n      if   ( s [ i ]   ===   ' '   &&   ++ spaceCount  ===  k )   return  s . slice ( 0 ,  i ) ; \n    } \n    return  s ; \n } ; Comments (3) vidamaleki Aug 09, 2021 I used your first method but I didn't use slice... \nvar truncateSentence = function(s, k) { \nreturn s.split(' ', k).join(' '); \n}; \n this works fine too 5 Show 5 Replies Reply jongourley99 Jun 19, 2021 You can decrement k and check for when it's 0 instead of using spaceCount. 1 Show 2 Replies Reply scraych Apr 27, 2021 Good. Nothing to add here. 0 Show 1 Replies Reply",
