      "2 Approach (solutions) | Fully Explained 💯 | Simple and Efficient Code 💯🔥 RockAdil 24 334 Aug 24, 2023 JavaScript Array Hash Table String 1+ 1. (Set and Reduce) Approach \n Intuition \n We're counting the number of words in an array that consist only of characters from a given set of allowed characters. \n Approach \n \n Convert the allowed characters into a set for quick lookup. \n For each word in the words array, split the word into individual characters. \n Check if every character in the word exists in the allowed set using the every function. \n If all characters are allowed, increment the count. \n Return the final count. \n \n Complexity \n \n Time complexity:  O(n * m) \n \n\n \n Space complexity:  O(m) \n \n\n Code \n var   countConsistentStrings   =   function   ( allowed ,  words )   { \n   allowed  =   new   Set ( allowed ) ; \n \n    return  words . reduce ( ( count ,  word )   =>   { \n      return  word . split ( '' ) . every ( l   =>  allowed . has ( l ) )   ?   ++ count  :  count ; \n    } ,   0 ) ; \n } ; \n \n \n 2. (Brute Force) Approach \n Intuition \n We're counting the number of words in an array that only contain characters from a given set of allowed characters. \n Approach \n \n Initialize a count variable to keep track of the consistent words. \n For each word in the words array:\n \n Initialize a consistent flag as true. \n For each letter in the word:\n \n Check if the letter is not included in the allowed        characters using the includes function. \n If not allowed, set consistent to false and break out of the loop. \n \n \n If consistent is still true, increment the count. \n \n \n Return the final count. \n \n Complexity \n \n Time complexity:  O(n * m) \n \n\n \n Space complexity:  O(1) \n \n\n Code \n var   countConsistentStrings   =   function   ( allowed ,  words )   { \n    let  count  =   0 ; \n \n    for   ( let  word  of  words )   { \n      let  consistent  =   true ; \n      for   ( let  letter  of  word )   { \n        if   ( ! allowed . includes ( letter ) )   { \n         consistent  =   false ; \n          break ; \n        } \n      } \n     consistent  ?  count ++   :   '' ; \n    } \n \n    return  count ; \n } ; \n \n \n Given the analysis, while this approach works, it has a higher time complexity compared to the first approach using  Set and reduce . Therefore, if performance is a concern, the first approach is generally preferred. 👍 \n If any doubts comment it.. ✨ \n Happy Coding :) \n <img src=\"https://assets.leetcode.com/users/images/770195a1-d985-4304-ac77-72768cc8d94b_1692887191.8325512.jpeg\" alt=\"upvote.jpeg\"> Comments (2) adilahamed189 Aug 24, 2023 Thanks bro!! 1 Reply Rickey07 Oct 18, 2023 First approach is good bro but generally it uses JS in built methods which interviewer or anyother person would not let us use 0 Show 1 Replies Reply"
