      "✅ Beats 100% - C++/Java/Python/JS - Chebyshev distance - Visualized Too lancertech6 5546 11105 Dec 02, 2023 C++ Java Python JavaScript 3+ <img src=\"https://assets.leetcode.com/users/images/e90d5999-64df-4e21-aa74-5fa6753906b1_1701570895.8401551.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2023-12-03 080131.png\"> \n YouTube Video Explanation: \n \n\n 🔥 Please like, share, and subscribe to support our channel's mission of making complex concepts easy to understand. \n Subscribe Link:  https://www.youtube.com/@leetlogics/?sub_confirmation=1 \n Current Subscribers: 553 \n Subscribe Goal: 600 Subscribers \n \n Example Explanation \n Let's create a step-by-step explanation using tables. \n Step 1: Define Helper Function \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n Function Name Parameters Description toTime from  (int[]),  to  (int[]) Calculates time to move from one point to another. Returns max(abs(xDiff), abs(yDiff)). \n Step 2: Initialize Variables \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n Variable Description time Accumulated time for traversal, initialized to 0. \n Step 3: Traverse Points \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n Iteration Current Points Time Calculation Accumulated Time Initial - - 0 1st [1,1] to [3,4] max(abs(1-3), abs(1-4)) = 3 3 2nd [3,4] to [-1,0] max(abs(3-(-1)), abs(4-0)) = 4 7 \n Step 4: Result \n The accumulated time after traversing all points is 7, which is the minimum time to visit all points. \n \n Intuition \n\n The problem essentially asks for the minimum time to traverse a sequence of points on a 2D plane following specific movement rules. We can calculate the time it takes to move between consecutive points and sum these times to get the total time. \n Approach \n\n \n We define a helper function  toTime  that calculates the time to move from one point to another. The time is the maximum of the absolute differences in x and y coordinates. \n We iterate through the points, starting from the second point (index 1), and accumulate the time using the  toTime  function. \n The final accumulated time is the answer. \n \n Complexity \n \n The time complexity is O(n), where n is the number of points, as we iterate through the points once. \n The space complexity is O(1) as we use a constant amount of extra space. \n \n Code \n Java C++ Python JavaScript var   toTime   =   function ( from ,  to )   { \n      var  xDiff  =   Math . abs ( from [ 0 ]   -  to [ 0 ] ) ; \n      var  yDiff  =   Math . abs ( from [ 1 ]   -  to [ 1 ] ) ; \n \n      return   Math . max ( xDiff ,  yDiff ) ; \n } ; \n var   minTimeToVisitAllPoints   =   function ( points )   { \n      var  time  =   0 ; \n \n      for   ( var  i  =   1 ;  i  <  points . length ;  i ++ )   { \n         time  +=   toTime ( points [ i  -   1 ] ,  points [ i ] ) ; \n      } \n \n      return  time ; \n } ; \n <img src=\"https://assets.leetcode.com/users/images/9a1b1ce3-daea-424a-9910-fea11e03ce61_1701570874.4181395.png\" alt=\"upvote.png\"> Comments (9) mathewmattson07 Dec 02, 2023 Dear Contributor, there have been many reports against your account of using other accounts to get popularity in solutions. It's been also observed that same accounts downvote other. A warning has been issued, if this behaviour continues your account will be taken down. \nBefore also your posts were taken down due to religious involvement. \n Please follow rules, or you will be debarred. 23 Reply jack_jackson Dec 02, 2023 Report this user 7 Reply nepia_0067 Dec 03, 2023 Current top post and 3rd post are written by the same author. \nIntuition, Approach, Complexity and solution codes are all the same as this post. \n I think this author has multiple accounts. \n https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-time-visiting-all-points/solutions/4356038/beats-101-1266-minimum-time-visiting-all-points-c-python-java/?envType=daily-question&envId=2023-12-03 6 Reply oskarsh Dec 02, 2023 Reported you. You haven't only generated a solution with ChatGPT (why would you put \"Certainly\" in it?), you've even generated a cat! 8 Reply jack_jackson Dec 02, 2023 https://leetcode.com/discuss/interview-question/4356361/Report-and-Petition-Against-User-Downvoting-Others 2 Reply dev_ved Dec 02, 2023 Check out easy approach  https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-time-visiting-all-points/solutions/4355727/easy-explained-c-efficient-simple-maths/ 1 Reply kailashsahu07 Dec 15, 2023 it is a realy a better solution 0 Reply Diff64 Dec 03, 2023 0 Reply mohd-usman Dec 03, 2023 Here is the detailed explanation with DRY run  https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-time-visiting-all-points/solutions/4357234/easiest-explanation-intuition-code-c/ 0 Reply",
      "✅100 % Beats Easy Clean Code ✅✅✅ sourav_n06 268 3256 Dec 02, 2023 C++ JavaScript Array Math 1+ Intuition \n\n Approach \n\n Complexity \n \n Time complexity: O(N) \n \n\n \n Space complexity: O(1) \n \n\n Code \n C++ JavaScript  \n var   minTimeToVisitAllPoints   =   function ( points )   { \n          let  ans  =   0 ; \n          for ( let  i  =   0   ;  i  <  points . length - 1 ;  i ++ ) \n          { \n              let  first  =   0 ; \n              let  second  =   0 ; \n              // Index (i, 0) \n              if   ( points [ i ] [ 0 ]   <   0   &&  points [ i + 1 ] [ 0 ]   <   0 ) \n                 first  =   Math . abs (   Math . abs ( points [ i ] [ 0 ] )   -   Math . abs ( points [ i + 1 ] [ 0 ] ) ) ; \n              else   if ( points [ i ] [ 0 ]   <   0   ||  points [ i + 1 ] [ 0 ]   <   0 ) \n                 first  =   Math . abs (   Math . abs ( points [ i ] [ 0 ] )   +   Math . abs ( points [ i + 1 ] [ 0 ] ) ) ; \n              else \n                 first  =   Math . abs (   Math . abs ( points [ i ] [ 0 ] )   -   Math . abs ( points [ i + 1 ] [ 0 ] ) ) ; \n              // Index (i, 1) \n              if ( points [ i ] [ 1 ]   <   0   &&  points [ i + 1 ] [ 1 ]   <   0 ) \n                 second  =   Math . abs (   Math . abs ( points [ i ] [ 1 ] )   -   Math . abs ( points [ i + 1 ] [ 1 ] ) ) ; \n              else   if ( points [ i ] [ 1 ]   <   0   ||  points [ i + 1 ] [ 1 ]   <   0 ) \n                 second  =   Math . abs (   Math . abs ( points [ i ] [ 1 ] )   +   Math . abs ( points [ i + 1 ] [ 1 ] ) ) ; \n              else \n                 second  =   Math . abs (   Math . abs ( points [ i ] [ 1 ] )   -   Math . abs ( points [ i + 1 ] [ 1 ] ) ) ; \n             ans  +=   Math . max ( first , second ) ; \n          } \n          return  ans ; \n } ; Comments (7) alex-mb Dec 03, 2023 One line solution  is also interesting 2 Reply sourav_n06 Dec 02, 2023 ✅100 % Beats Easy Clean Code ✅✅✅ \n https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-time-visiting-all-points/solutions/4355589/100-beats-easy-clean-code/ \nClick Here to Check ->✅ 2 Reply ahmedkhairym0 Dec 03, 2023 Why all of if statements? 1 Reply jack_jackson Dec 02, 2023 https://leetcode.com/discuss/interview-question/4356361/Report-and-Petition-Against-User-Downvoting-Others 1 Reply ultra_plusplus Dec 02, 2023 It doesn't beat 100% why lying? 0 Show 1 Replies Reply rampageousrj Dec 15, 2023 whats the need for checking less than zero condition if youre already using abs() function? 0 Reply mohd-usman Dec 03, 2023 Here is the detailed explanation with DRY run  https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-time-visiting-all-points/solutions/4357234/easiest-explanation-intuition-code-c/ 0 Reply",
      "【Video】Give me 5 minutes - How we think about a solution niits 10815 608 Dec 03, 2023 C++ Java Python3 JavaScript Intuition \n We can move diagonally. \n \n Solution Video \n \n ■ Timeline of the video \n 0:05  Think about distance of two points with a simple example \n 1:37  Demonstrate how it works \n 3:31  Make sure important points \n 5:09  Coding \n 6:26  Time Complexity and Space Complexity \n 6:36  Step by step algorithm with my solution code \n ⭐️⭐️ Don't forget to subscribe to my channel! ⭐️⭐️ \n ■ Subscribe URL \n http://www.youtube.com/channel/UC9RMNwYTL3SXCP6ShLWVFww?sub_confirmation=1 \n Subscribers: 3,357 \nMy first goal is 10,000 (It's far from done 😅) \nThank you for your support! \n My channel reached 3,000 subscribers these days. Thank you so much for your support! \n \n Approach \n How we think about a solution \n Let's think about a simple case. \n <img src=\"https://assets.leetcode.com/users/images/60e52351-cd1c-4321-9655-cd8103b642bf_1701614480.5848057.png\" alt=\"スクリーンショット 2023-12-03 23.41.02.png\"> \n Start point is bottom left of  red  and goal point is top right of  blue . we can move only 4 corners of each cell(top left, top right, bottom left and bottom right). In this case, how many seconds do we need? \n The answer is  2  seconds. \n \n If we move  bottom left of red  →  top right of red  →  top right of blue , we need 2 seconds. \n Other path need more seconds. For example, \n bottom left of red  →  bottom right of red  →  bottom right of yellow  →  top right of yellow  →  top right of blue \n We need 4 seconds. \n \n How about this? \n <img src=\"https://assets.leetcode.com/users/images/afa33ba2-249c-4178-b2dc-c41b5ca6676e_1701614802.3379517.png\" alt=\"スクリーンショット 2023-12-03 23.46.19.png\"> \n Again start point is bottom left of  red  and goal point is top right of  blue . In this case, how many seconds do we need? \n \n One of paths for minimum seconds should be  bottom left of red  →  top right of red  →  top right of orange  →  top right of blue \n Other path need more seconds. For example, \n bottom left of red  →  bottom right of red  →  bottom right of yellow  →  top right of yellow  →  top right of orange  →  top right of blue \n We need 5 seconds. \n \n \n Why these happen? \n \n That's because we can move diagonally, that means we can move vertically and horizontally at the same time. We can save seconds. \n In other words, \n \n ⭐️ Points \n Moving the same distance as the greater difference between the horizontal coordinates (x-axis) or vertical coordinates (y-axis) of two points is sufficient. \n \n In the second example above, we move 3 points vertically and 2 points horizontally. So we need to move 3 times to reach the goal point(= top right of blue). \n Let's see the example in the description. \n Input: points = [[1,1],[3,4],[-1,0]] \n Since we need to take greater difference between 2 points, we start from index 1. We use absolute value. \n abs ( 3  -  1 )  vs abs ( 4  -  1 )   ( =  horizontal distance vs vertical distance ) \n =  2  vs  3 \n =  3  seconds \n Next 2 points \n abs ( -1  -  3 )  vs abs ( 0  -  4 )   ( =  horizontal distance vs vertical distance ) \n =  4  vs  4 \n =  4  seconds \n Total seconds should be \n Output: 7 (seconds) \n Easy!😄 \nLet's see a real algorithm. \n \n Algorithm Overview: \n The algorithm calculates the minimum time required to visit all points on a 2D plane. It iterates through the given list of points and accumulates the maximum difference in horizontal or vertical coordinates between consecutive points. \n Detailed Explanation: \n \n \n Initialize Result Variable: \n res  =   0 \n \n res  is initialized to 0. This variable will store the accumulated minimum time to visit all points. \n \n \n \n Iterate through Points: \n for  i  in   range ( 1 ,   len ( points ) ) : \n \n The algorithm uses a for loop to iterate through the points starting from index 1 (since there is no need to calculate the distance for the first point). \n \n \n \n Calculate Horizontal and Vertical Differences: \n res  +=   max ( abs ( points [ i ] [ 0 ]   -  points [ i  -   1 ] [ 0 ] ) ,  \n              abs ( points [ i ] [ 1 ]   -  points [ i  -   1 ] [ 1 ] ) ) \n \n For each pair of consecutive points, the algorithm calculates the absolute differences in horizontal and vertical coordinates. \n abs(points[i][0] - points[i - 1][0])  calculates the absolute horizontal difference. \n abs(points[i][1] - points[i - 1][1])  calculates the absolute vertical difference. \n \n \n \n Accumulate Maximum Difference: \n res  +=   max ( . . . ) \n \n The algorithm adds the maximum of the horizontal and vertical differences to the  res  variable. This ensures that the accumulated time represents the maximum distance traveled in either the horizontal or vertical direction between consecutive points. \n \n \n \n Return Result: \n return  res \n \n The final result, representing the minimum time to visit all points, is returned. \n \n \n \n Overall, the algorithm efficiently calculates the minimum time required to visit all points by considering the maximum difference in either the horizontal or vertical direction between consecutive points. \n \n Complexity \n \n \n Time complexity:  O(n)O(n) O ( n ) \nn is the number of points in the input array. The loop iterates through the points array once, and each iteration involves constant time operations. \n \n \n Space complexity:  O(1)O(1) O ( 1 ) \n \n \n Python JavaScript Java C++  \n var   minTimeToVisitAllPoints   =   function ( points )   { \n      let  res  =   0 ; \n \n      for   ( let  i  =   1 ;  i  <  points . length ;  i ++ )   { \n         res  +=   Math . max ( \n              Math . abs ( points [ i ] [ 0 ]   -  points [ i  -   1 ] [ 0 ] ) , \n              Math . abs ( points [ i ] [ 1 ]   -  points [ i  -   1 ] [ 1 ] ) \n          ) ; \n      } \n \n      return  res ;     \n } ; \n \n Thank you for reading my post. \n⭐️ Please upvote it and don't forget to subscribe to my channel! \n ■ Subscribe URL \n http://www.youtube.com/channel/UC9RMNwYTL3SXCP6ShLWVFww?sub_confirmation=1 \n ■ Twitter \n https://twitter.com/CodingNinjaAZ \n My previous daily coding challenge post and video. \n post \n https://leetcode.com/problems/find-words-that-can-be-formed-by-characters/solutions/4354470/video-give-me-5-minutes-how-we-think-about-a-solution/ \n video \n \n ■ Timeline of the video \n 0:05  How we can solve \"Find Words That Can Be Formed by Characters\" \n 3:48  What if we have extra character when specific character was already 0 in HashMap? \n 4:17  Coding \n 7:45  Time Complexity and Space Complexity \n 8:17  Step by step algorithm with my solution code Comments (1) Shreyas_G_S Feb 17, 2024 0 Reply",
      "[JavaScript] Easy to understand poppinlp 1703 1254 Nov 28, 2019 JavaScript Since we can move to 8 directions, the only thing we need that calculate the minimum movements is that the larger distance in vertical and horizontal between 2 points. \n const   minTimeToVisitAllPoints   =   points   =>   { \n    let  times  =   0 ; \n    for   ( let  i  =   1 ;  i  <  points . length ;   ++ i )   { \n      const  p0  =  points [ i  -   1 ] ; \n      const  p1  =  points [ i ] ; \n     times  +=   Math . max ( Math . abs ( p1 [ 0 ]   -  p0 [ 0 ] ) ,   Math . abs ( p1 [ 1 ]   -  p0 [ 1 ] ) ) ; \n    } \n    return  times ; \n } ; \n Time complexity: O(n) \nSpace complexity: O(1) Comments (2) hamidhamri Dec 15, 2022 awesome 0 Show 1 Replies Reply AbdulKhader4123 Apr 14, 2021 awesome 0 Show 1 Replies Reply",
      "✅☑[C++/Java/Python/JavaScript] || Easy Solution || EXPLAINED🔥 MarkSPhilip31 8512 931 Dec 02, 2023 C++ Java Python3 C 4+ PLEASE UPVOTE IF IT HELPED \n \n Approaches \n (Also explained in the code) \n \n \n minTimeToVisitAllPoints Function: \n \n This function calculates the minimum time required to visit all points given a vector of points. \n \n \n \n Total Time Calculation: \n \n It initializes the  ans  variable to keep track of the total time. \n It iterates through the points starting from the second point (index 1) to compare consecutive points. \n For each pair of consecutive points, it calculates the absolute differences in x and y coordinates ( diffx  and  diffy ). \n It adds the maximum of these differences ( max(diffx, diffy) ) to the total time  ans . \n \n \n \n Complexity \n \n \n Time complexity: \n O(n)O(n) O ( n ) \n \n \n Space complexity: \n O(1)O(1) O ( 1 ) \n \n \n Code \n C++ C Java Python3 JavaScript var   minTimeToVisitAllPoints   =   function ( points )   { \n      let  ans  =   0 ; \n      const  n  =  points . length ; \n \n      for   ( let  i  =   1 ;  i  <  n ;   ++ i )   { \n          const  diffx  =   Math . abs ( points [ i ] [ 0 ]   -  points [ i  -   1 ] [ 0 ] ) ; \n          const  diffy  =   Math . abs ( points [ i ] [ 1 ]   -  points [ i  -   1 ] [ 1 ] ) ; \n \n         ans  +=   Math . max ( diffx ,  diffy ) ; \n      } \n \n      return  ans ; \n } ; \n \n \n \n \n PLEASE UPVOTE IF IT HELPED \n \n \n Comments (1) alex-mb Dec 03, 2023 One line solution  is also interesting 1 Reply"
