      "Python/JS/Go/C++ O(n) by linear scan. [w/ Comment] 有中文解題文章 brianchiang_tw 6894 6495 Jun 21, 2020 C++ Python Python3 C 4+ 中文解題文章 \n O(n) by linear scan. \n \n Hint : \n Maintain a record of first largest number as well as second largest \n \n Implementation : \n Python: \n class Solution ( object ) :\n     def maxProduct ( self ,  nums ) :\n \n          first ,   second   =   0 ,   0 \n         \n          for  number  in  nums:\n             \n              if  number  >   first :\n                  # update first largest and second largest \n                  first ,   second   =  number ,   first \n                 \n             elif number  >   second :\n                  # update second largest \n                  second   =  number\n         \n          return   ( first   -   1 )   *   ( second   -   1 ) \n \n Javascript: \n var   maxProduct   =   function ( nums )   { \n     \n      let   [ first ,  second ]   =   [ 0 ,   0 ] ; \n     \n      for (   const   number   of  nums ) { \n         \n          if (   number   >  first  ) { \n              [ first ,  second ]   =   [ number ,  first ] ; \n             \n          } else   if (   number   >  second  ) { \n             second  =   number ; \n          } \n      }  \n     \n      return   (  first  -   1   )   *   (  second  -   1   ) ; \n     \n } ; \n \n Go: \n func   maxProduct ( nums  [ ] int )   int   { \n     \n     first ,  second  :=   0 ,   0 \n     \n      for   _ ,  number  :=   range  nums { \n         \n          if  number  >  first { \n             first ,  second  =  number ,  first\n             \n          } else   if  number  >  second  { \n             second  =  number\n          } \n      } \n     \n      return   (  first  -   1   ) * (  second  -   1   ) \n } \n \n C++ \n class   Solution   { \n public : \n      int   maxProduct ( vector < int > &  nums )   { \n         \n          int  first  =   0 ,  second  =   0 ; \n         \n          for (   const   int &  number :  nums ) { \n             \n              if (  number  >  first  ) { \n                 second  =  first ; \n                 first  =  number ; \n                 \n              } else   if (  number  >  second  ) { \n                 second  =  number ; \n              } \n             \n          } \n         \n          return   (  first  -   1   )   *   (  second  -   1   ) ; \n      } \n } ; Comments (9) leihao1313 Jan 04, 2021 7 Show 1 Replies Reply pforderique Dec 21, 2020 great! I was unsure of how to keep track of 2 max numbers while iterating only once through the list --- great, readable code, thanks! 2 Show 1 Replies Reply bartekpacia Sep 24, 2020 I love this, very pythonic! 2 Show 1 Replies Reply saideepsamineni Jun 08, 2022 2 Show 1 Replies Reply Rehman1998 Jun 20, 2021 easy to read !! 1 Show 1 Replies Reply ggplay Oct 10, 2020 1 Show 2 Replies Reply dapilk101 Dec 12, 2023 Read more 0 Reply uzzbb Oct 11, 2023 O(n) 0 Reply msustar May 22, 2021 -7 Reply",
      "[JavaScript] Easy to understand - 3 solutions poppinlp 1703 2605 Jun 01, 2020 JavaScript Since the integers in  nums  are in range  [1, 10^3] , the key to this problem is to find out the biggest and the second biggest number in  nums . \n SOLUTION 1 \n We use 2 variables to maintain our targets during the traversal of  nums . It's pretty straight forward. \n const   maxProduct   =   nums   =>   { \n    let  m1  =   0 ,  m2  =   0 ; \n    for   ( const  val  of  nums )   { \n     m2  =   Math . max ( m2 ,   Math . min ( m1 ,  val ) ) ; \n     m1  =   Math . max ( m1 ,  val ) ; \n    } \n    return   ( m1  -   1 )   *   ( m2  -   1 ) ; \n } ; \n SOLUTION 2 \n We use 2 pointers - one starts from 0 and the other one starts from  nums.length - 1  - to traversal to the middle. \n During the traversal, we maintain current biggest number in  i  or  j . If  i  is smaller, we do  ++i . If  j  is smaller, we do  --j . \n So, when the traversal finished, we must have met this situation - the biggest number and the second biggest number have been in  i  and  j . \n const   maxProduct   =   nums   =>   { \n    let  max  =   0 ; \n    for   ( let  i  =   0 ,  j  =  nums . length   -   1 ;  i  <  j ; )   { \n     max  =   Math . max ( max ,   ( nums [ i ]   -   1 )   *   ( nums [ j ]   -   1 ) ) ; \n     nums [ i ]   <  nums [ j ]   ?   ++ i  :   -- j ; \n    } \n    return  max ; \n } ; \n SOLUTION 3 \n We use the  Array.prototype.reduce  to make solution 1 to an 1-line version. \n const  maxProduct  =   ( nums ,  max  =  nums . reduce ( ( prev ,  val )   =>   [ Math . max ( prev [ 1 ] ,   Math . min ( prev [ 0 ] ,  val ) ) ,   Math . max ( prev [ 0 ] ,  val ) ] ,   [ 0 ,   0 ] ) )   =>   ( max [ 0 ]   -   1 )   *   ( max [ 1 ]   -   1 ) ; Comments (4) mrhyde Oct 30, 2020 4 Show 1 Replies Reply jennhuynh02 Sep 04, 2020 I've noticed that you always have multiple solutions in your post - thank you! 2 Reply changhyoshon100 Jan 15, 2023 You are genius! 0 Show 1 Replies Reply Beyonder19 Apr 23, 2021 Great!!! 0 Show 1 Replies Reply",
      "【Video】Give me 5 minutes - 2 solutions - How we think about a solution niits 10815 4114 Dec 11, 2023 C++ Java Python3 JavaScript Intuition \n Keep the largest number so far \n \n Solution Video \n \n ■ Timeline of the video \n 0:05  Explain algorithm of Solution 1 \n 3:18  Coding of solution 1 \n 4:18  Time Complexity and Space Complexity of solution 1 \n 4:29  Step by step algorithm of solution 1 \n 4:36  Explain key points of Solution 2 \n 5:25  Coding of Solution 2 \n 6:50  Time Complexity and Space Complexity of Solution 2 \n 7:03  Step by step algorithm with my stack solution code \n ⭐️⭐️ Don't forget to subscribe to my channel! ⭐️⭐️ \n ■ Subscribe URL \n http://www.youtube.com/channel/UC9RMNwYTL3SXCP6ShLWVFww?sub_confirmation=1 \n Subscribers: 3,424 \nMy first goal is 10,000 (It's far from done 😅) \nThank you for your support! \n \n Approach \n How we think about a solution \n Seems like all numbers are positive, so simply all we have to do is to find the two largest numbers in input array. \n The key point of my first solution is \n \n ⭐️ Points \n Keep the largest number(=  cur_max  in the solution code) so far and compare \n res vs  ( cur_max  -  1 )  *  ( nums [ i ]  -  1 ) \n res  is current max result so far \n nums[i]  is current number \n \n Let's see one by one. \n Input: nums = [1,5,4,5] \n We keep the number at index 0 as a current max number. \n res =  0 \n cur_max =  1   ( =  nums [ 0 ] ) \n Start iteration from index 1. \n At   index   1 ,   Compare \n \n max ( res ,   ( cur_max   -   1 )   *   ( nums [ i ]   -   1 ) ) \n →  max ( 0 ,   ( 1   -   1 )   *   ( 5   -   1 ) ) \n =   0 \n \n res   =   0 \n res  is still  0  and we need to update  cur_max  if current number is greater than  cur_max . \n cur_max  =   max ( cur_max ,  nums [ i ] ) \n =   1  vs  5 \n \n cur_max  =   5 \n I'll speed up. \n At   index   2 ,   Compare \n \n max ( res ,   ( cur_max   -   1 )   *   ( nums [ i ]   -   1 ) ) \n →  max ( 0 ,   ( 5   -   1 )   *   ( 4   -   1 ) ) \n =   12 \n \n res   =   12 \n cur_max   =   5   ( =   max ( 5 ,   4 ) ) \n At   index   3 ,   Compare \n \n max ( res ,   ( cur_max   -   1 )   *   ( nums [ i ]   -   1 ) ) \n →  max ( 12 ,   ( 5   -   1 )   *   ( 5   -   1 ) ) \n =   16 \n \n res   =   16 \n cur_max   =   5   ( =   max ( 5 ,   5 ) ) \n Output: 16 \n Easy 😆! \nLet's see a real algorithm! \n \n Complexity \n \n Time complexity:  O(n)O(n) O ( n ) \n \n\n \n Space complexity:  O(1)O(1) O ( 1 ) \n \n\n Python JavaScript Java C++  \n var   maxProduct   =   function ( nums )   { \n      let  res  =   0 ; \n      let  curMax  =  nums [ 0 ] ; \n \n      for   ( let  i  =   1 ;  i  <  nums . length ;  i ++ )   { \n         res  =   Math . max ( res ,   ( curMax  -   1 )   *   ( nums [ i ]   -   1 ) ) ; \n         curMax  =   Math . max ( curMax ,  nums [ i ] ) ; \n      } \n \n      return  res ;     \n } ; \n Step by step algorithm \n Initialization: \n res  =   0 \n cur_max  =  nums [ 0 ] \n In this section,  res  is initialized to 0, and  cur_max  is initialized to the first element of the  nums  list. These variables will be used to keep track of the maximum product and the maximum value encountered during the iterations, respectively. \n Iteration through the List: \n for  i  in   range ( 1 ,   len ( nums ) ) : \n     res  =   max ( res ,   ( cur_max  -   1 )   *   ( nums [ i ]   -   1 ) ) \n     cur_max  =   max ( cur_max ,  nums [ i ] ) \n In this section, the code iterates through the list starting from the second element (index 1). For each element at index  i : \n \n res  is updated to the maximum of its current value and the product of  (cur_max - 1) * (nums[i] - 1) . This ensures that  res  always holds the maximum product found so far. \n cur_max  is updated to the maximum of its current value and the current element  nums[i] . This ensures that  cur_max  always holds the maximum value encountered in the list up to the current index. \n \n Return Result: \n return  res \n After the loop completes, the final value of  res  represents the maximum product of two distinct elements in the list. The function returns this maximum product. \n \n Solution 2 \n Basically, we try to find the first max and second max numbers. \n \n ⭐️ Points \n If current number is greater than the first max number so far, then \n current  number will be the  first  max number\n current   first  max number will be  second  max number \n If current number is less than the first max number so far, then compare \n second  max number  =   max ( second  max number ,   current  number ) \n Because there is still possibility that current number will be the second max number. \n \n At last, we multiply the first and second max numbers. \n Easy!😄 \nLet's see a real algorithm \n \n Complexity \n \n Time complexity:  O(n)O(n) O ( n ) \n \n\n \n Space complexity:  O(1)O(1) O ( 1 ) \n \n\n Python JavaScript Java C++ var   maxProduct   =   function ( nums )   { \n      let  firstMax  =   0 ; \n      let  secondMax  =   0 ; \n \n      for   ( let  num  of  nums )   { \n          if   ( num  >  firstMax )   { \n              [ secondMax ,  firstMax ]   =   [ firstMax ,  num ] ; \n          }   else   { \n             secondMax  =   Math . max ( secondMax ,  num ) ; \n          } \n      } \n \n      return   ( firstMax  -   1 )   *   ( secondMax  -   1 ) ;     \n } ; \n Step by step algorithm \n \n Initialization: \n \n first_max, second_max = 0, 0 \n \n first_max  and  second_max  are initialized to 0. These variables will be used to keep track of the two largest elements in the list. \n \n \n Iteration through the List: \n \n for  num  in  nums : \n      if  num  >  first_max : \n         second_max ,  first_max  =  first_max ,  num\n      else : \n         second_max  =   max ( second_max ,  num ) \n \n The code iterates through each element  num  in the  nums  list. \n For each  num :\n \n If  num  is greater than  first_max , it means  num  is the new maximum. Therefore, the values of  second_max  and  first_max  are updated accordingly. This is done using the simultaneous assignment technique in Python:  second_max, first_max = first_max, num . \n If  num  is not greater than  first_max  but is greater than  second_max , it means  num  is the new second maximum. Therefore,  second_max  is updated using  second_max = max(second_max, num) . \n \n \n \n \n Return Result: \n \n return   ( first_max  -   1 )   *   ( second_max  -   1 ) \n \n After the loop completes, the function returns the product of  (first_max - 1)  and  (second_max - 1) . This is because the task is to find the maximum product of two distinct elements, so we subtract 1 from each of the largest elements before multiplying. \n \n The algorithm efficiently finds the two largest elements in the list and returns their product minus 1. \n \n Thank you for reading my post. \n⭐️ Please upvote it and don't forget to subscribe to my channel! \n ■ Subscribe URL \n http://www.youtube.com/channel/UC9RMNwYTL3SXCP6ShLWVFww?sub_confirmation=1 \n ■ Twitter \n https://twitter.com/CodingNinjaAZ \n My next daily coding challenge post and video. \n post \n https://leetcode.com/problems/special-positions-in-a-binary-matrix/solutions/4397604/video-give-me-5-minutes-how-we-think-about-a-solution/ \n video \n \n ■ Timeline of the video \n 0:05  Explain algorithm of the first step \n 1:06  Explain algorithm of the second step \n 2:36  Coding \n 4:17  Time Complexity and Space Complexity \n 4:55  Step by step algorithm of my solution code \n My previous daily coding challenge post and video. \n post \n https://leetcode.com/problems/element-appearing-more-than-25-in-sorted-array/solutions/4388310/video-give-me-5-minutes-2-solutions-how-we-think-about-a-solution/ \n video \n \n ■ Timeline of the video \n 0:07  Explain algorithm of Solution 1 \n 2:46  Coding of solution 1 \n 3:49  Time Complexity and Space Complexity of solution 1 \n 4:02  Step by step algorithm of solution 1 \n 4:09  Explain key points of Solution 2 \n 4:47  Explain the first key point \n 6:06  Explain the second key point \n 7:40  Explain the third key point \n 9:33  Coding of Solution 2 \n 14:20  Time Complexity and Space Complexity of Solution 2 \n 14:48  Step by step algorithm with my stack solution code Comments (4) maria_q Dec 12, 2023 May be useful to have a look at  several simple oneliners  for this task. 1 Reply sourav_n06 Dec 11, 2023 ✅Beats 100 % Easy Clean Code✅✅✅ \n https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-product-of-two-elements-in-an-array/solutions/4392999/beats-100-easy-clean-code/ \nClick Here Right Now ✅ 0 Reply diyavj Dec 12, 2023 Can't we just sort the array and use last two elements? 0 Show 2 Replies Reply minamisuna Dec 11, 2023 Thanks for multiple solutions! 0 Reply",
      "✅☑[C++/Java/Python/JavaScript] || 3 Approaches || Beats 100% || EXPLAINED🔥 MarkSPhilip31 8512 3665 Dec 11, 2023 C++ Java Python3 C 4+ PLEASE UPVOTE IF IT HELPED \n \n Approaches \n (Also explained in the code) \n Approach 1(Brute Force) \n \n The function iterates through the elements in the  nums  array using two nested loops ( i  and  j ) to consider all possible pairs of elements. \n For each pair of elements ( nums[i], nums[j] ), it calculates the product of  (nums[i] - 1) * (nums[j] - 1) . \n It updates the  ans  variable to hold the maximum product found among all pairs considered. \n Finally, it returns the maximum product of two distinct elements in the array. \n \n Complexity \n \n \n Time complexity: \n O(n2)O(n^2) O ( n 2 ) \n \n \n Space complexity: \n O(1)O(1) O ( 1 ) \n \n \n Code \n C++ C Java Python3 JavaScript function   maxProduct ( nums )   { \n      let  ans  =   0 ; \n \n      for   ( let  i  =   0 ;  i  <  nums . length ;  i ++ )   { \n          for   ( let  j  =  i  +   1 ;  j  <  nums . length ;  j ++ )   { \n             ans  =   Math . max ( ans ,   ( nums [ i ]   -   1 )   *   ( nums [ j ]   -   1 ) ) ; \n          } \n      } \n \n      return  ans ; \n } \n \n \n Approach 2(Sorting) \n \n The function sorts the input array  nums  in ascending order using  sort(nums.begin(), nums.end()) . \n It then retrieves the largest element ( x ) and the second largest element ( y ) from the sorted array. \n The function returns the product of  (x - 1) * (y - 1) , which is the maximum possible product of two distinct elements in the array after subtracting 1 from each of the two largest elements. \n \n Complexity \n \n \n Time complexity: \n O(nlogn)O(nlogn) O ( n l o g n ) \n \n \n Space complexity: \n O(logn)O(logn) O ( l o g n )  or  O(n)O(n) O ( n ) \n \n \n Code \n C++ C Java Python3 JavaScript function   maxProduct ( nums )   { \n     nums . sort ( ( a ,  b )   =>  a  -  b ) ; \n \n      const  x  =  nums [ nums . length   -   1 ] ; \n      const  y  =  nums [ nums . length   -   2 ] ; \n \n      return   ( x  -   1 )   *   ( y  -   1 ) ; \n } \n \n \n Approach 3(Second Biggest) \n \n The function  maxProduct  iterates through each element ( num ) in the  nums  array. \n It maintains two variables,  biggest  and  secondBiggest , to keep track of the largest and second largest numbers encountered in the array. \n If the current  num  is greater than the current  biggest , it updates  secondBiggest  to the previous value of  biggest  and updates  biggest  to the current  num . \n If the current  num  is not greater than  biggest , it updates  secondBiggest  if necessary (keeping track of the second largest number encountered). \n Finally, it returns the product of  (biggest - 1) * (secondBiggest - 1) , which is the maximum product of two distinct elements in the array after subtracting 1 from each of the two largest elements. \n \n Complexity \n \n \n Time complexity: \n O(n)O(n) O ( n ) \n \n \n Space complexity: \n O(1)O(1) O ( 1 ) \n \n \n Code \n C++ C Java Python3 JavaScript function   maxProduct ( nums )   { \n      let  biggest  =   Number . MIN_SAFE_INTEGER ;   // Variable to store the largest number in 'nums' \n      let  secondBiggest  =   Number . MIN_SAFE_INTEGER ;   // Variable to store the second largest number in 'nums' \n \n      for   ( let  i  =   0 ;  i  <  nums . length ;  i ++ )   {   // Iterating through each number in 'nums' \n          if   ( nums [ i ]   >  biggest )   { \n              // If the current number is greater than 'biggest', \n              // update 'secondBiggest' and 'biggest' \n             secondBiggest  =  biggest ; \n             biggest  =  nums [ i ] ; \n          }   else   { \n              // If the current number is not greater than 'biggest', \n              // update 'secondBiggest' if necessary (keeping track of the second largest) \n             secondBiggest  =   Math . max ( secondBiggest ,  nums [ i ] ) ; \n          } \n      } \n \n      return   ( biggest  -   1 )   *   ( secondBiggest  -   1 ) ;   // Return the product of (biggest - 1) and (secondBiggest - 1) \n } \n \n \n PLEASE UPVOTE IF IT HELPED \n \n \n Comments (4) maria_q Dec 12, 2023 May be useful to have a look at  several simple oneliners  for this task. 1 Reply sourav_n06 Dec 11, 2023 ✅Beats 100 % Easy Clean Code✅✅✅ \n https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-product-of-two-elements-in-an-array/solutions/4392999/beats-100-easy-clean-code/ \nClick Here Right Now ✅ 0 Reply Vishwajeet_Pawar Dec 11, 2023 why ,Collections.reverseOrder() does not work in leetcode ? I tried but showed error \n import java.util.Collections; class Solution {     public int maxProduct(int[] nums) {         Arrays.sort(nums,Collections.reverseOrder());         return (nums[0]-1)*(nums[1]-1) ;     } } \nLine 4: error: no suitable method found for sort(int[],Comparator) \nArrays.sort(nums,Collections.reverseOrder()); \n^ \nmethod Arrays.<T#1>sort(T#1[],Comparator<? super T#1>) is not applicable \n(inference variable T#1 has incompatible bounds \nequality constraints: int \nlower bounds: Object) \nmethod Arrays.<T#2>sort(T#2[],int,int,Comparator<? super T#2>) is not applicable \n(cannot infer type-variable(s) T#2 \n(actual and formal argument lists differ in length)) \nwhere T#1,T#2 are type-variables: \nT#1 extends Object declared in method <T#1>sort(T#1[],Comparator<? super T#1>) \nT#2 extends Object declared in method <T#2>sort(T#2[],int,int,Comparator<? super T#2>) Read more 0 Reply shubham_savaj Dec 11, 2023 ✅Best Method 🔥|| Beat 100% Runtime & 95.28% Memory|| C++ || Beginner Friendly🔥🔥🔥 \n https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-product-of-two-elements-in-an-array/solutions/4393986/best-method-beat-100-runtime-95-28-memory-c-beginner-friendly/ -1 Reply"
