      "✅☑Beats 100% Users || [C++/Java/Python/JavaScript] || 2 Approaches || EXPLAINED🔥 MarkSPhilip31 8512 25978 Feb 13, 2024 C++ Java Python Python3 4+ DO GIVE IT A LIKE IF THAT WAS HELPFUL🫡👇 \n \n <img src=\"https://assets.leetcode.com/users/images/9821f906-c979-45b7-ab5f-ccc9726f15bc_1707871931.2888937.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2024-02-14 054522.png\"> \n \n Approaches \n (Also explained in the code) \n Approach 1 ( Brute Force ) \n \n We will iterate over the  nums  vector. \n If we encounter positive number then putting in  v1  and if negative putting in  v2 . \n \n <img src=\"https://assets.leetcode.com/users/images/af9b0df1-19f6-46b3-8be2-c6712a8ffc80_1707871345.196443.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2024-02-14 060503.png\"> \n \n With the help of while loop iterating both together and putting values in the  ans  vector, where  ind1  in index for  v1  and  ind2  for  v2  and the 1st element should be positive. \n \n NOTE🗒🚀 \n Here, we're appending values sequentially thayswhy used push_back()\n \n \n Complexity \n \n \n Time complexity: \n O(n)O(n) O ( n ) \n \n \n Space complexity: \n O(n)O(n) O ( n ) \n \n \n Code \n C++ Java Python3 JavaScript var   Solution   =   function ( )   { } ; \n \n Solution . prototype . rearrangeArray   =   function ( nums )   { \n      let  v1  =   [ ] ; \n      let  v2  =   [ ] ; \n      let  ans  =   [ ] ; \n     \n      for   ( let  num  of  nums )   { \n          if   ( num  >   0 )   { \n             v1 . push ( num ) ; \n          }   else   { \n             v2 . push ( num ) ; \n          } \n      } \n     \n      let  ind1  =   0 ; \n      let  ind2  =   0 ; \n     \n      while   ( ind2  <  nums . length   /   2 )   { \n         ans . push ( v1 [ ind1 ] ) ; \n         ind1 ++ ; \n         ans . push ( v2 [ ind2 ] ) ; \n         ind2 ++ ; \n      } \n     \n      return  ans ; \n } ; \n \n \n \n Approach 2( Optimized ) \n \n Here we are directly putting values in the  ans  vector with the help of 2 pointer approach where initially both the indexes  pos  and  neg  are  0  and  1 . \n If we encounter positive element then will put values in the  ans  vector with the help of  ans[pos] = nums[i]  and if found negative element then  ans[neg] = nums[i] . \n \n <img src=\"https://assets.leetcode.com/users/images/77e41419-855a-4589-be76-5cc62e098401_1707871575.2027254.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2024-02-14 061156.png\"> \n NOTE🗒🚀 \n Here, we can't use ans.push_back() to add elements because\n  we're not appending values sequentially. Instead, we're placing\n  values at specific indexes based on the element we have.\n \n The number 1....6 are the order in which the elements where\n  pushed into the ans vector.\n \n \n Complexity \n \n \n Time complexity: \n O(n)O(n) O ( n ) \n \n \n Space complexity: \n O(n)O(n) O ( n ) \n \n \n Code \n C++ Java Python3 JavaScript \n var   Solution   =   function ( )   { } ; \n \n Solution . prototype . rearrangeArray   =   function ( nums )   { \n      let  ans  =   new   Array ( nums . length ) . fill ( 0 ) ; \n      let  pos  =   0 ,  neg  =   1 ; \n     \n      for   ( let  i  =   0 ;  i  <  nums . length ;  i ++ )   { \n          if   ( nums [ i ]   >   0 )   { \n             ans [ pos ]   =  nums [ i ] ; \n             pos  +=   2 ; \n          }   else   { \n             ans [ neg ]   =  nums [ i ] ; \n             neg  +=   2 ; \n          } \n      } \n     \n      return  ans ; \n } ; \n \n \n DO GIVE IT A LIKE IF THAT WAS HELPFUL🫡👇 \n \n \n Comments (14) lc00703 Feb 13, 2024 *3 Ways to solve it => (Two Pass -> One Pass -> In-place swap O(1) space) \n www.youtube.com/watch?v=tMl-su7ALDo 5 Show 2 Replies Reply priyankachowdhury2002 Feb 13, 2024 Thanks for sharing ! Here are 2 approaches explained under 5 mins :) \n Read more 2 Reply utkarshbajpaii Feb 20, 2024 nice problem explaination 1 Show 1 Replies Reply OneOfAll Feb 14, 2024 I used the 2nd approach exactly as you mention ( no I didnt copy ) but my runtime was 1926ms. I do not understand how solutions with 2 loops can have better runtime than my solution with 1 loop and less memory usage. 1 Show 1 Replies Reply chetannada Feb 14, 2024 nice solution with full explanation 1 Show 1 Replies Reply abhisek169 Feb 14, 2024 The first approach is easy and other one was too, just need attention. Thanks for sharing. 1 Show 1 Replies Reply ankit485803 Feb 14, 2024 right 1 Show 1 Replies Reply maria_q Feb 14, 2024 There is  one line solution  for this task - may be interesting! 1 Reply zinchse Feb 13, 2024 it isn't acceptable. we should  rearrange  initial array. please, read description 1 Show 5 Replies Reply meg1709 May 09, 2024 Nice optimal solution approach! 0 Reply",
      "✅☑Beats 100% Users || Easy Understood Solution with optimized space || 2 Approaches🔥 MindOfshridhar 541 732 Feb 14, 2024 C++ Java Python JavaScript 6+ <img src=\"https://assets.leetcode.com/users/images/9b63b513-a863-443c-988c-b0cb543825de_1707900692.0013316.png\" alt=\"13.PNG\"> \n Complexity \n \n Time complexity: O(n) \n \n\n \n Space complexity: O(n) \n \n\n Code 1 \n Explanation: \n \n The rearrangeArray function takes a list of integers (nums) as input and rearranges its elements based on their sign. \n Two lists, n for negative numbers and p for positive numbers, are initialized to separate the input numbers based on their sign. \n Iterating through the input nums, each element is appended to either the n or p list depending on whether it's negative or positive. \n Three pointers, i, j, and k, are initialized to 0. i and j are used to iterate through the negative (n) and positive (p) lists, respectively, while k is used to iterate through the original list (nums). \n In a while loop, elements from the n and p lists are interleaved into the original nums list based on the parity of the index k.\n \n If k is even, a positive number from p is placed at the current index in nums. \n If k is odd, a negative number from n is placed at the current index in nums. \n \n \n The pointers i, j, and k are updated accordingly. \n After interleaving, if there are remaining elements in either n or p, they are appended to the end of nums. \n The modified nums list is then returned as the final result. \n \n Python Java c++ JavaScript  \n var   rearrangeArray   =   function ( nums )   { \n      // Separate negative (n) and positive (p) numbers into two arrays \n      const  n  =   [ ] ; \n      const  p  =   [ ] ; \n      for   ( const  i  of  nums )   { \n          if   ( i  <   0 )   { \n             n . push ( i ) ; \n          }   else   { \n             p . push ( i ) ; \n          } \n      } \n \n      // Initialize pointers for iterating through n, p, and nums \n      let  i  =   0 ,  j  =   0 ,  k  =   0 ; \n \n      // Interleave positive and negative numbers in the original nums array \n      while   ( i  <  n . length   &&  j  <  p . length )   { \n          // If the current index is even, place a positive number from p \n          if   ( k  %   2   ===   0 )   { \n             nums [ k ]   =  p [ j ] ; \n             j ++ ; \n          } \n          // If the current index is odd, place a negative number from n \n          else   { \n             nums [ k ]   =  n [ i ] ; \n             i ++ ; \n          } \n         k ++ ; \n      } \n \n      // If there are remaining negative numbers, append them to nums \n      while   ( i  <  n . length )   { \n         nums [ k ]   =  n [ i ] ; \n         i ++ ; \n         k ++ ; \n      } \n \n      // If there are remaining positive numbers, append them to nums \n      while   ( j  <  p . length )   { \n         nums [ k ]   =  p [ j ] ; \n         j ++ ; \n         k ++ ; \n      } \n \n      // Return the modified nums array with interleaved positive and negative numbers \n      return  nums ; \n } ; \n \n \n Code 2 🔥 \n Python Java C++ Javascript class   Solution ( object ) : \n      def   rearrangeArray ( self ,  nums ) : \n          \"\"\"\n         :type nums: List[int]\n         :rtype: List[int]\n         \"\"\" \n          # Calculate the length of the input list \n         n  =   len ( nums ) \n         \n          # Initialize a result list with placeholders for rearranged elements \n         ans  =   [ - 1 ]   *  n\n         \n          # Initialize indices for positive and negative elements in the result list \n         posind  =   0 \n         negind  =   1 \n         \n          # Iterate through the input nums \n          for  i  in  nums : \n              # If the number is positive, place it at the even index in the result list \n              if  i  >   0 : \n                 ans [ posind ]   =  i\n                 posind  +=   2 \n              # If the number is negative, place it at the odd index in the result list \n              else : \n                 ans [ negind ]   =  i\n                 negind  +=   2 \n         \n          # Return the rearranged result list \n          return  ans      \n <img src=\"https://assets.leetcode.com/users/images/bdb0d3fb-d0a8-46f0-bb6c-8eb313564cc0_1707899877.739647.jpeg\" alt=\"leetcode up1.png\"> Comments (1) maria_q Feb 14, 2024 There is  one line solution  for this task - may be interesting! 0 Reply",
      "🌟 JavaScript || Professional Approach and In-Depth Explanation withaarzoo 607 724 Feb 13, 2024 JavaScript Intuition \n Given an array of integers, we need to rearrange the elements such that positive integers appear first, followed by negative integers, with relative ordering preserved within each group. \n Approach \n \n Initialize two arrays to store positive and negative numbers separately. \n Traverse the input array and categorize each number into the appropriate array based on its sign. \n Merge the positive and negative arrays alternatively into the result array. \n Return the rearranged array. \n \n Complexity \n \n Time complexity:\n \n The time complexity of the approach is O(n), where n is the number of elements in the input array. This is because we traverse the input array once to separate positive and negative numbers, and then perform a constant-time operation for merging. \n \n \n Space complexity:\n \n The space complexity is also O(n) as we create separate arrays to store positive and negative numbers, and the space required for the result array is also proportional to the size of the input array. \n \n \n \n Code \n  \n var   rearrangeArray   =   function ( nums )   { \n      // Create two arrays to store positive and negative numbers \n      const  pos  =   [ ] ;   // array for positive numbers \n      const  neg  =   [ ] ;   // array for negative numbers \n     \n      // Separate positive and negative numbers \n      for   ( const  num  of  nums )   { \n          if   ( num  >   0 )   { \n             pos . push ( num ) ;   // if the number is positive, add it to the positive array \n          }   else   { \n             neg . push ( num ) ;   // if the number is non-positive (including zero), add it to the negative array \n          } \n      } \n     \n      // Merge positive and negative arrays alternatively \n      const  res  =   new   Array ( nums . length ) ; \n      let  k  =   0 ;   // index for the result array \n      let  i  =   0 ;   // index for the positive array \n      let  j  =   0 ;   // index for the negative array \n     \n      // Alternate between positive and negative arrays until one of them is exhausted \n      while   ( i  <  pos . length   &&  j  <  neg . length )   { \n         res [ k ++ ]   =  pos [ i ++ ] ;   // add a positive number \n         res [ k ++ ]   =  neg [ j ++ ] ;   // add a negative number \n      } \n     \n      // Add remaining elements from the positive array, if any \n      while   ( i  <  pos . length )   { \n         res [ k ++ ]   =  pos [ i ++ ] ; \n      } \n     \n      // Add remaining elements from the negative array, if any \n      while   ( j  <  neg . length )   { \n         res [ k ++ ]   =  neg [ j ++ ] ; \n      } \n     \n      return  res ; \n } ; \n This approach provides a straightforward and efficient solution to the problem, with a clear explanation of the logic and complexities involved. Comments (10) maria_q Feb 14, 2024 There is  one line solution  for this task - may be interesting! 1 Reply priyankachowdhury2002 Feb 13, 2024 Thanks for sharing ! Here are 2 approaches explained under 5 mins :) \n Read more 1 Reply Slicer9875 Feb 13, 2024 I don't see the advantage compared to just using 2 pointers and allocating a single array. 1 Reply lc00703 Feb 13, 2024 *3 Ways to solve it => (Two Pass -> One Pass -> In-place swap O(1) space) \n www.youtube.com/watch?v=tMl-su7ALDo 0 Reply cs_iitian Feb 13, 2024 Interview Approach - Follow Up Questions \n https://leetcode.com/problems/rearrange-array-elements-by-sign/solutions/4723827/interview-approach-follow-up-question/ 0 Reply nusratjahannj018982 Feb 13, 2024 Great Explanation 🔥 0 Show 1 Replies Reply dgisight Feb 13, 2024 🔥🔥 0 Show 1 Replies Reply witharonus Feb 13, 2024 🌟🌟 0 Show 1 Replies Reply user5275EM Feb 13, 2024 🔥🔥 0 Show 1 Replies Reply arolearn04 Feb 13, 2024 Great 🔥 0 Show 1 Replies Reply",
      "Short JavaScript Solution sronin 348 881 Mar 11, 2022 JavaScript Found this solution helpful? Consider showing support by upvoting this post. If there are any questions, kindly leave a comment below. Thank you and happy hacking! \n var   rearrangeArray   =   function   ( nums )   { \n      let  positiveNums  =  nums . filter ( x   =>  x  >   0 ) \n      let  negativeNums  =  nums . filter ( x   =>  x  <   0 ) \n      let  result  =   [ ] \n \n      for   ( let  i  =   0 ;  i  <  nums . length   /   2 ;  i ++ )   { \n         result . push ( positiveNums [ i ] ,  negativeNums [ i ] ) \n      } \n \n      return  result\n } ; Comments (2) dsuptest Jan 24, 2023 Forgot you can push two numbers at a time. Clever! 2 Reply anubhav_13 Jul 13, 2023 why we are we using  i < nums.length/2 0 Show 1 Replies Reply",
      "【Video】Do you know this Python code? See Bonus code. niits 10815 281 Feb 13, 2024 C++ Java Python3 JavaScript Intuition \n Two indices for positive numbers and negative numbers. \n Solution Video \n \n ⭐️⭐️ Don't forget to subscribe to my channel! ⭐️⭐️ \n ■ Subscribe URL \n http://www.youtube.com/channel/UC9RMNwYTL3SXCP6ShLWVFww?sub_confirmation=1 \n Subscribers: 3,877 \nMy first goal is 10,000 (It's far from done 😅) \nThank you for your support! \n \n Approach \n Simply, we know that positive numbers start from index 0 and negative numbers start from index 1. \n Input: nums = [3,1,-2,-5,2,-4] \n Create  res  and  two indices  for positive and negative numbers. \n res = [0,0,0,0,0,0]\n pi = 0 (= positive index)\n ni = 1 (= negative index) \n Let's iterate through one by one. \n [ 3 , 1 , - 2 , - 5 , 2 , - 4 ] \n  ↑ \n We found positive number. Current positive index(= pi) is index 0, so we put  3  at index 0. \n res = [3,0,0,0,0,0] \n After that, we know that next positive position should be index 2, because we have to put numbers like postive, negative, positive, negative...that's why  add +2 to pi \n res = [3,0,0,0,0,0]\n pi = 2\n ni = 1 \n I'll speed up. \n [ 3 , 1 , - 2 , - 5 , 2 , - 4 ] \n    ↑\n \n Found positive  number \n \n res  =   [ 3 , 0 , 1 , 0 , 0 , 0 ] \n \n pi  =   4   ( add   + 2   to  pi ) \n ni  =   1 \n [ 3 , 1 , - 2 , - 5 , 2 , - 4 ] \n       ↑\n \n Found negative  number \n \n res  =   [ 3 , - 2 , 1 , 0 , 0 , 0 ] \n \n pi  =   4 \n ni  =   3   ( add   + 2   to  ni ) \n [ 3 , 1 , - 2 , - 5 , 2 , - 4 ] \n          ↑\n \n Found negative  number \n \n res  =   [ 3 , - 2 , 1 , - 5 , 0 , 0 ] \n \n pi  =   4 \n ni  =   5   ( add   + 2   to  ni ) \n [ 3 , 1 , - 2 , - 5 , 2 , - 4 ] \n            ↑\n \n Found positive  number \n \n res  =   [ 3 , - 2 , 1 , - 5 , 2 , 0 ] \n \n pi  =   6   ( add   + 2   to  pi ) \n ni  =   5 \n [ 3 , 1 , - 2 , - 5 , 2 , - 4 ] \n               ↑\n \n Found negative  number \n \n res  =   [ 3 , - 2 , 1 , - 5 , 2 , - 4 ] \n \n pi  =   6  \n ni  =   7   ( add   + 2   to  pi ) \n return   [ 3 , - 2 , 1 , - 5 , 2 , - 4 ] \n Easy😄! \nLet's see solution codes and step by step algorithm! \n ⭐️ I recently created a video on how I've been using LeetCode to learn. \n \n \n Complexity \n \n Time complexity: O(n) \n \n\n \n Space complexity: O(n) \n \n\n Python JavaScript Java C++  \n var   rearrangeArray   =   function ( nums )   { \n      let  pi  =   0 ; \n      let  ni  =   1 ; \n      let  res  =   new   Array ( nums . length ) . fill ( 0 ) ; \n \n      for   ( let  n  of  nums )   { \n          if   ( n  >   0 )   { \n             res [ pi ]   =  n ; \n             pi  +=   2 ; \n          }   else   { \n             res [ ni ]   =  n ; \n             ni  +=   2 ; \n          } \n      } \n \n      return  res ;     \n } ; \n Step by step algorithm \n \n \n Initialization: \n pi  =   0 \n ni  =   1 \n res  =   [ 0 ]   *   len ( nums ) \n \n pi  and  ni  are initialized to 0 and 1 respectively. These variables will be used as pointers to track the positions of positive and negative integers in the result array. \n res  is initialized as a list of zeros with the same length as the input  nums . \n \n \n \n Iteration through  nums : \n for  n  in  nums : \n \n This loop iterates through each element  n  in the input array  nums . \n \n \n \n Assignment for Positive Integers: \n if  n  >   0 : \n     res [ pi ]   =  n\n     pi  +=   2 \n \n If the current element  n  is greater than 0 (positive), it's assigned to the position indicated by the  pi  pointer in the result array  res . \n After the assignment, the  pi  pointer is incremented by 2 to skip the next position for the next positive integer. \n \n \n \n Assignment for Negative Integers: \n else : \n     res [ ni ]   =  n\n     ni  +=   2 \n \n If the current element  n  is less than or equal to 0 (negative), it's assigned to the position indicated by the  ni  pointer in the result array  res . \n After the assignment, the  ni  pointer is incremented by 2 to skip the next position for the next negative integer. \n \n \n \n Return Result Array: \n return  res \n \n After processing all elements in the input array, the resulting array  res  is returned. \n \n \n \n \n Bonus code \n In Python, we can write like this. Create positive list and negative list and mix them together. \n Python class   Solution : \n      def   rearrangeArray ( self ,  nums :  List [ int ] )   - >  List [ int ] : \n         pos ,  neg  =   [ ] ,   [ ] \n \n          for  n  in  nums : \n              if  n  >   0 : \n                 pos . append ( n ) \n              else : \n                 neg . append ( n ) \n         \n         res  =   [ 0 ]   *   len ( nums ) \n         res [ 0   :   len ( pos )   *   2   :   2 ]   =  pos\n         res [ 1   :   len ( neg )   *   2   :   2 ]   =  neg\n \n          return  res \n \n Thank you for reading my post. \n⭐️ Please upvote it and don't forget to subscribe to my channel! \n ■ Subscribe URL \n http://www.youtube.com/channel/UC9RMNwYTL3SXCP6ShLWVFww?sub_confirmation=1 \n ■ Twitter \n https://twitter.com/CodingNinjaAZ \n ⭐️ Yesterday's daily question. \n \n ⭐️ I solved the one of weekly content problems. \n Comments (2) maria_q Feb 14, 2024 There is  one line solution  for this task - may be interesting! 1 Reply tvatsa Feb 13, 2024 It's actually O(1) in space because the space taken by output is not counted towards the space consumption. 0 Reply"
